ed experiment_id.\"}"}], "assistant_content": "{\n  \"experiment_id\": \"angular_refined\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Angular under-resolution is likely the dominant cause of ray artifacts, as coarse angular sampling (n_mu=2, n_phi=16) cannot capture directionally varying flux features. Increasing to 32 projected directions (n_mu=4, n_phi=32) should mitigate this without increasing the computational cost associated with spatial refinement.\",\n  \"decision_reason\": \"The previous coarse experiment used only 2 polar and 16 azimuthal angles, which is insufficient for resolving anisotropic transport. Ray artifacts often stem from insufficient angular quadrature, leading to visible discontinuities. Refining angular resolution is relatively inexpensive compared to spatial refinement and directly tests the angular under-resolution hypothesis. Spatial refinement alone (position_refined) would leave angular artifacts unaffected, while combined refinement is more costly. Therefore, angular_refined is the best single next experiment.\"\n}", "response_id": "0bf6663d-4066-4906-89f8-708ca8196437", "system_fingerprint": "a307abda487cd1b463329ccb945ce396", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 467, "completion_tokens": 189, "total_tokens": 656, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens": 0, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 467}, "secret_recorded": false}
